[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Lee et al., 2018-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d49aaa352d4d714f564ea6d8f79b51f558f42ef9)
- venue: Nature Genetics
- citation count: 2196
[method]
The paper used large-scale genome-wide association studies for educational attainment and related cognitive phenotypes, including cognitive performance, math ability, and highest math. It applied conditional and joint multiple-SNP analysis to identify independent association signals, a Bayesian winner's-curse-adjusted posterior effect-size framework, replication of lead SNPs, cross-cohort genetic correlation, and multi-trait analysis of GWAS to combine cognitive phenotypes. Polygenic scores, including the EduYears score, were constructed and benchmarked for predictive power. SNP filtering removed variants with strand issues, invalid P values, minor allele count below 25, poor imputation, indels or non-autosomal locations, invalid or duplicated coordinates, allele mismatches, and, for UK Biobank full-release results, variants absent from the Haplotype Reference Consortium reference panel. This relates to the proposed method's use of PGS from large-scale cognitive GWAS as a genetic proxy, but the paper does not use the ABCD predictive modeling, opportunistic stacking, or commonality analyses with mental health.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not report quantitative outcomes such as number of genome-wide significant loci, effect sizes, p-values, replication statistics, genetic correlations, or polygenic prediction R2/correlations. It only states that no data were excluded beyond standard SNP quality-control filters and that statistical parameters should be confirmed in figure legends or Methods. Therefore, no specific numerical result can be extracted from the supplied text.
[context]
The experimental context is a GWAS and polygenic prediction study of educational attainment and cognitive phenotypes, with UK Biobank full-release data filtered to the Haplotype Reference Consortium reference panel. The excerpt does not provide sample sizes, computational hardware, model hyperparameters, or evaluation metrics. For the proposed research plan, the relevant condition is that the genetic proxy would be derived from large-scale GWAS summary statistics after standard QC, rather than from the ABCD sample or from the paper's predictive modeling pipeline.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 2]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Choi et al., 2020-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c28110039f725c98fe48a687cca1b853d0588fbf)
- venue: Nature Protocols
- citation count: 1667
[method]
The paper describes a polygenic risk score construction and evaluation workflow that is directly relevant to the proposed cognitive polygenic score proxy. It recommends quality control of both base GWAS and target genotype data, including standard GWAS checks such as SNP genotyping rate, minor allele frequency, imputation information score, individual genotyping rate, and file-integrity checks. For score construction, the paper contrasts two broad shrinkage strategies: statistical shrinkage of all SNP effects using methods such as LASSO, ridge regression, or Bayesian priors, and P-value threshold selection, in which only SNPs below a chosen GWAS P-value threshold are included and all other SNPs are effectively assigned zero effect. The P-value threshold approach is treated as a tuning or variable-selection procedure: because the optimal threshold is unknown in advance, scores are computed across multiple thresholds and the most predictive score is selected. The paper also emphasizes overfitting control in PRS association testing. It states that selecting the most predictive score across many thresholds can overfit the target sample, and recommends out-of-sample prediction by optimizing parameters in a training set and testing in an independent validation set. If an independent set is unavailable, it recommends splitting the target sample into training and validation subsets or using repeated cross-validation. When validation data are absent or underpowered, it recommends permutation-based empirical P-values for association testing. This relates to the proposed research plan because the plan uses three cognitive polygenic score definitions, selects optimal PGS thresholds within training sets, applies Elastic Net prediction, and uses nested leave-one-site-out cross-validation with internal 10-fold tuning before extracting predicted values for commonality analyses.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not report a final numeric predictive accuracy, R-squared, correlation, or P-value for a cognitive polygenic score or for the proposed proxy-measure pipeline. The only quantitative experimental outcome described is a power and accuracy evaluation design for PRS using traits with estimated heritabilities of 0.49, 0.23, and 0.11. In that design, each trait sample was split into 100,000 base individuals and 100,000 target individuals, with validation samples ranging from 50 to 3,000 individuals, and each analysis was repeated five times with independently selected validation samples. The substantive methodological result is that the most predictive P-value threshold is not a fixed indicator of the fraction of causal variants; it depends on the effect-size distribution, base and target sample power, genetic architecture, and causal-variant fraction. The paper notes that underpowered GWAS data can make a P-value threshold of 1, meaning all SNPs, optimal even when only a small fraction of SNPs is causal. It also states that association P-values for an optimized PRS should be interpreted as empirical P-values estimated from the target data rather than as nominal regression P-values.
[context]
