[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Morris et al., 2021-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b6365360938e3ff69bdb19f5809c3f4b578136f6)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 18
[method]
The paper used elastic net regression within a nested participant-level cross-validation framework to predict adherence to exercise interventions. Inputs included 136 structural MRI features derived from FreeSurfer cortical thickness and surface area, functional connectivity degree-count features from a 300-node Schaefer atlas computed at 15%, 20%, and 25% connection thresholds, and psychosocial, physical function, activity, health, and cognitive features. Before modeling, features not correlated with adherence at p < 0.1 were removed, and age, biological sex, and mean framewise displacement for functional features were regressed out. The outer loop was 10-fold cross-validation used to estimate generalization performance on held-out participants, while the inner loop was another 10-fold cross-validation with grid search to select elastic net hyperparameters lambda1 and lambda2. The optimal functional degree-count threshold was selected by training models at each threshold and choosing the threshold with the smallest outer-fold RMSE. Three models were trained: imaging only, non-imaging psychosocial/physical/health/cognitive only, and multimodal all features. For the non-imaging model, nested elastic net failed, so a simple 10-fold cross-validated Ridge regression model was used. This method relates to the proposed nested leave-one-site-out validation because both use an inner tuning loop and an outer evaluation loop to avoid hyperparameter leakage, but the paper used random participant folds rather than site-level folds, so it does not directly test multi-site generalizability across the 21 ABCD sites.
[results]
All three models significantly predicted adherence. The multimodal model achieved R2 = 0.22, RMSE = 0.10, MAE = 0.09, and p < 0.001. The imaging-only model achieved R2 = 0.15, RMSE = 0.11, MAE = 0.09, and p < 0.001. The psychosocial/cognitive/health-only model achieved R2 = 0.06, RMSE = 0.12, MAE = 0.10, and p = 0.001. Significance was assessed using 1000 nonparametric permutations comparing the observed R2 to a permuted null distribution. Selected multimodal features included positive functional degree-count nodes in default mode, dorsal attention, and frontoparietal control networks, negative nodes in somatomotor, visual, temporoparietal, and ventral attention regions, positive cortical thickness and negative cortical surface area features, and psychosocial/cognitive features such as gait self-efficacy, perceived stress, sleep quality, biological sex, and letter comparison. These outcomes show that nested cross-validated regularized regression can extract modest but significant predictive signal from high-dimensional neuroimaging and non-neuroimaging features, supporting the proposed plan's use of nested validation for predictive proxies, although the paper's effect sizes come from a single-site adherence prediction task rather than ABCD cognitive-mental health modeling.
[context]
The study included N = 131 participants, mean age 65.79 years, 71.6% female, 89.7% White, mean education 15.89 years, and mean adherence 81%. Power analysis estimated 96% power to detect an effect size of 0.22 with 38 covariates in the multimodal model, while a sensitivity sample of N = 59 had 60% power. Structural preprocessing used FreeSurfer v5.3, and functional preprocessing used CONN v19c with SPM12, 2 mm functional voxels, 6 mm smoothing, 0.01-0.1 Hz band-pass filtering, CompCor denoising, and motion regression. Functional degree count was computed at fixed network costs of 15%, 20%, and 25%, with the threshold selected by outer-fold RMSE. Reported optimal hyperparameters were lambda1 = n/a and lambda2 = 100 for the psychosocial/cognitive/health Ridge model, lambda1 = 0.173 and lambda2 = 100 for the imaging elastic net model, and lambda1 = 0.194 and lambda2 = 89 for the multimodal elastic net model. Analyses were run in RStudio 3.6.3 using dplyr, purrr, penalized, pensim, and ggplot2, and no specific hardware or cluster resources were reported. The key constraint relative to the proposed method is that the paper's validation was participant-level within one dataset and did not include leave-one-site-out testing, site-specific training, or the 21-site ABCD structure; therefore it provides a methodological precedent for nested inner-10-fold hyperparameter tuning and outer performance evaluation, but not direct evidence for cross-site generalizability.
[end related work 1]

